  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nH5 upper low continues moving east across the Plains and into the \nMidwest this afternoon. Most of eastern North Dakota and \nnorthwestern Minnesota is seeing mostly sunny skies as a result, \nwith only some mid level cloud cover in west central Minnesota. \nHighs this afternoon will reach the low to mid 60s. For tonight, \nlows are expected to fall into the lower 40s across much of the \narea, with the possibility for middle to upper 30s in portions of \nnorthwest Minnesota. Similar conditions prevail on Tuesday with \nhighs in the 60s, perhaps approaching 70 degrees in the northern Red \nRiver Valley. Our next chance for precipitation enters the forecast \nlate Wednesday into Thursday, with additional rain chances this \nweekend.\n\n...Frost Possible Tonight into Tuesday Morning...\n\nLow temperatures tonight are expected to fall into the middle 30s to \nupper 30s, primarily in northwest Minnesota (60 percent chance), \nwith a lower chance (20 percent) in the northern Red River Valley \nand far northeast North Dakota. Radiational cooling is expected to \nbe efficient as winds go nearly calm tonight across the area. Dew \npoints will generally be in the middle to upper 30s as well, thus \nlimiting the floor of lower temps. Patchy ground fog could form in \ncooler spots around sunrise.",
